export const posts=[{"id": "woods", "title": "From Maple Groves to Redwood Canopies", "date": "November 21, 2024", "tag": "WRITING", "excerpt": "Full text from Stephanie Gao_Muir Woods.pdf", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"writing/woods.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read the original PDF ↗</a> · Full text, including references and assignment notes. Figures are available in the PDF.</p><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 1\"><p>GL91 - Engagement Across Semester</p><p>From Maple Groves to Redwood Canopies</p><p>Minerva University</p><p>GL91 Global Learning</p><p>Dean Lindo</p><p>November 21, 2024</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 2\"><p>SELF-DIRECTED - MUIR WOODS</p><p>The idea for the camping trip to Muir Woods came to me one afternoon when I was</p><p>sitting in my dorm, scrolling through photos of my time at Pearson College in Canada. I had</p><p>spent years surrounded by dense forests, hiking through backcountry trails, and building</p><p>boardwalks to protect old-growth trees. Nature had been my constant companion, and the silence</p><p>of the forest had been my refuge. But since moving to the city, I felt a growing distance from that</p><p>side of me—the outdoorsy, adventure-seeking Canadian girl who lived for the depths of the</p><p>woods. While I love the city, I needed to reconnect, and that’s when I decided to organize the trip</p><p>for fall break.</p><p>I invited a group of friends, all of whom had never really gone camping before. I was</p><p>eager to show them another side of me—the one who thrived in nature, who could build a fire</p><p>with a few sticks and set up a tent in the dark. I wanted them to experience the peace that comes</p><p>from being surrounded by towering trees, the sense of adventure that comes with hiking, and the</p><p>simplicity of cooking oatmeal over an open flame. I hoped it would be a chance for all of us to</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 3\"><p>step away from the chaos of city life and immerse ourselves in something that felt much more</p><p>grounded.</p><p>When we arrived in Muir Woods, it felt like stepping into another world. The towering</p><p>redwoods, the soft crunch of fallen leaves beneath our feet, the smell of damp earth and moss—it</p><p>was all so familiar, yet it felt like I hadn’t experienced it in years. As we hiked deeper into the</p><p>forest, I couldn’t help but feel a rush of nostalgia. This was the kind of place where I had spent</p><p>so many weekends back in Canada, building boardwalks in rain or shine, hoping to protect the</p><p>ancient forests. On the first day, we hiked to a nearby beach, following trails that wove through</p><p>lush ferns and beneath the towering canopy. As I led the way, I shared stories of summiting</p><p>mountains and exploring thousand-year-old forests in Canada, my friends hanging on every</p><p>word. Yet, what stayed with me most wasn’t their fascination with my tales but the wonder in</p><p>their eyes—the unmistakable spark of experiencing the magic of forests first hand.</p><p>As we settled around the campfire that night, I reflected on how much I had missed this</p><p>part of myself—the side that wasn’t defined by schoolwork or city life, but by the simple joys of</p><p>being in nature. Sharing that with my friends, seeing them experience it for the first time, felt like</p><p>a gift. I realized that not only had I been reconnecting with nature, but I had also been</p><p>reconnecting with a part of me that I had forgotten. The forest, the trees, the trails—it all</p><p>reminded me of the importance of protecting these spaces, of doing the work to preserve what is</p><p>still wild and untouched.</p><p>WORD COUNT: 498 WORDS</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 4\"><p>AI STATEMENT</p><p>AI usage was minimal in this process. I used tools like Grammarly and Google Spell</p><p>Check for basic grammar and spelling corrections.</p><p>For the reflection, I chose to use this opportunity to dig deep into a profound and</p><p>meaningful experience, writing a personal narrative to capture my thoughts and emotions</p><p>authentically.</p></section>"}, {"id": "political-analysis", "title": "Power to the People: An Analysis of the “No Kings” Protest Movement (2025–2026)", "date": "April 21, 2026", "tag": "WRITING", "excerpt": "Full text from SS112 FINAL ASSIGNMENT.docx.pdf", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"writing/political-analysis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read the original PDF ↗</a> · Full text, including references and assignment notes. Figures are available in the PDF.</p><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 1\"><p>SS112 - Final Assignment: Political Analysis</p><p>Power to the People: An Analysis of the “No Kings” Protest Movement (2025-2026)</p><p>Minerva University</p><p>SS112 - Political Science and Social Change</p><p>Professor L. Odera</p><p>April 21, 2026</p><p>Word Count: 1,278</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 2\"><p>Abstract</p><p>This paper analyzes the “No Kings” protest movement in the United States from 2025 to</p><p>2026 using structuralist and constructivist frameworks. Drawing on data from the Armed</p><p>Conflict Location and Event Data Project (ACLED), the World Values Survey (WVS) Wave 7,</p><p>the Carnegie Endowment for International Peace, and a range of journalistic and scholarly</p><p>sources, it finds that large-scale mobilization emerged from a combination of declining</p><p>institutional trust, heightened political polarization, and reinforcing interactions between state</p><p>responses and protest activity. At the same time, the movement’s anti-monarchical framing</p><p>helped unify diverse grievances into a shared democratic identity, sustaining participation across</p><p>three waves totaling approximately 20 million participants. The analysis suggests that despite its</p><p>scale, the movement’s long-term impact depends on its ability to translate civic mobilization into</p><p>electoral and institutional change.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 3\"><p>I. Introduction and Data Overview</p><p>In June 2025, the United States experienced one of the largest protest movements in its</p><p>history. The “No Kings” movement, named in explicit opposition to what organizers framed as</p><p>monarchical executive overreach, mobilized between 4 and 8 million participants across three</p><p>peak protest days, repeatedly broke its own participation records, and extended to all 50 states</p><p>(NoKings, 2026; Brittanica, 2026). This scale of participation raises a central question: Why did</p><p>the No Kings movement emerge in three distinct waves, and expand to such a large scale? This</p><p>paper examines the movement through structuralist and constructivist lenses, analyzing the</p><p>material conditions that enabled protest and the meanings that sustained it.</p><div class=\"essay-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td>Date</td><td>Event</td><td>General Significance</td></tr><tr><td>Late 2024 – Early 2025</td><td>Grievances intensified across key policy domains, including immigration enforcement, expanded executive authority, economic pressures (rising cost of living), and declining institutional responsiveness (Britannica, 2026)</td><td>Establishes structural preconditions by eroding institutional legitimacy and increasing polarization, creating a population more likely to engage in extra-institutional action</td></tr><tr><td>June 14, 2025</td><td>First “No Kings” protests coincide with U.S. Army 250th Anniversary Parade and President Trump’s birthday (Britannica, 2026)</td><td>Functions as a symbolic trigger event, linking existing grievances to a clear narrative of executive overreach and catalyzing coordinated mobilization</td></tr><tr><td>June 14, 2025</td><td>Nationwide protests across approximately 2,100 sites with</td><td>Marks initial large-scale mobilization, demonstrating the capacity of pre-existing</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 4\"><div class=\"essay-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td></td><td>4–6 million participants (Britannica, 2026)</td><td>networks to rapidly coordinate mass participation</td></tr><tr><td>Summer 2025</td><td>Continued unrest, including immigration raids and National Guard deployments (Britannica, 2026)</td><td>Illustrates early stages of the state–protest feedback dynamic, where state responses increase grievance salience and sustain mobilization</td></tr><tr><td>October 18, 2025</td><td>Second wave of protests across approximately 2,700 sites with around 7 million participants (Britannica, 2026)</td><td>Represents escalation in scale and geographic diffusion, indicating that mobilization expanded beyond initial participants</td></tr><tr><td>Late 2025</td><td>Intensified political conflict, including government shutdown and expanded federal intervention (Britannica, 2026)</td><td>Reinforces perceptions of institutional instability and executive overreach, further activating grievance-based mobilization</td></tr><tr><td>March 28, 2026</td><td>Third wave of protests across approximately 3,300 sites with around 8 million participants (Britannica, 2026)</td><td>Peak mobilization phase, reflecting non-linear escalation driven by reinforcing structural dynamics</td></tr><tr><td>2026 (ongoing)</td><td>Continued protests without major policy or leadership change (Britannica, 2026)</td><td>Indicates limits of protest impact under existing institutional constraints and persistence of underlying structural grievances</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Table 1. Timeline of the “No Kings” Protests in the United States (2024–Present). This</p><p>table outlines the sequence of structural conditions, triggering events, and successive waves of</p><p>mobilization that shaped the No Kings movement. The timeline serves as a reference framework</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 5\"><p>for the analysis that follows and is used in later sections to examine structural preconditions,</p><p>feedback dynamics, and patterns of non-linear escalation.</p><p>ACLED data confirm (See Table 1) that No Kings III (March 28, 2026) set a new record</p><p>with more than 2,400 single-day demonstrations nationwide (Doyle [ACLED], April 20261).</p><p>Across all three waves, cumulative participation reached approximately 20 million people,</p><p>representing about 2.3% of the U.S. population at peak engagement (Britannica, 2026; Doyle</p><p>[ACLED], April 2026).</p><p>Figure 1. ACLED choropleth map (No Kings I; June 14, 2025) showing demonstration</p><p>frequency by U.S. state. Darker shading indicates higher event density. California leads with 187</p><p>demonstrations in a single-day snapshot. The near-universal geographic distribution contradicts</p><p>the claim that opposition to the Trump administration was limited to coastal urban centers.</p><p>(Source: ACLED, 2025)</p><p>1 The month is included in the citation because the reports share the same title but are published for different months and years, making them easier to distinguish.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 6\"><p>Figure 2. ACLED map of large peaceful demonstration sites during No Kings I (June 14,</p><p>2025). Blue shading indicates states with National Guard deployments. State escalation did not</p><p>suppress protest but amplified it by reinforcing the movement’s executive-overreach narrative.</p><p>This pattern is examined in later sections through a structuralist account of state–protest</p><p>feedback dynamics and a constructivist analysis of norm reinforcement (Source: ACLED, 2025).</p><p>Geographically, the protests were both concentrated and widely distributed. As Figure 1</p><p>shows, states such as California recorded the highest number of demonstrations, while protests</p><p>also occurred across all 50 states, including traditionally conservative regions such as Wyoming,</p><p>South Dakota, and Alabama, indicating broad national reach (ACLED, 2025). In addition,</p><p>ACLED data indicate that over 99% of events did not involve violence, with fewer than 1%</p><p>classified as involving clashes or police intervention. Given the scale of mobilization, this</p><p>reflects a highly disciplined pattern of predominantly nonviolent civic participation (ACLED,</p><p>2025).</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 7\"><p>II. Structural Analysis</p><p>Structuralism posits that political behavior is primarily shaped by enduring systemic</p><p>constraints, including institutional arrangements, class relations, and distributions of power,</p><p>rather than by the choices or intentions of individual actors (Shively, 2022). The No Kings</p><p>movement did not arise from isolated individual decisions to protest; it emerged from structural</p><p>conditions that made large-scale mobilization a predictable outcome. Three key mechanisms can</p><p>be identified.</p><p>Legitimacy Erosion as a Standing Condition</p><p>World Values Survey (WVS) Wave 7 data (2017–2020) indicate low confidence in core</p><p>representative institutions prior to the protests, with roughly three-quarters of respondents</p><p>expressing a lack of confidence in the federal government. At the same time, about one-third</p><p>supported strong executive leadership even when it may bypass democratic procedures (WVS,</p><p>2020; PBS, 2026). From a structuralist perspective, these distributions point to longer-term</p><p>perceptions of institutional performance across areas such as healthcare, immigration, and</p><p>economic security, increasing the likelihood that grievances are expressed through (informal)</p><p>protest when catalyzed by political events.</p><p>Organizational Infrastructure as Structural Capacity</p><p>Protest does not arise from grievance alone; it depends on organizational infrastructure,</p><p>particularly when formal institutional channels are perceived as unresponsive. By early 2025,</p><p>declining confidence in government had already made civil society organizations the primary</p><p>vehicles for collective action. Groups such as Indivisible, 50501, MoveOn, the ACLU, and the</p><p>American Federation of Teachers formed a coalition of more than 200 organizations, enabling</p><p>coordination across local and national levels. The approximately 500 “Hands Off” protests in</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 8\"><p>April 2025 demonstrate that this network was operational prior to the emergence of No Kings</p><p>(Doyle [ACLED], August 2025).</p><p>State-Protest Feedback Dynamics</p><p>Building on declining institutional trust and pre-existing organizational infrastructure, the</p><p>“wave” pattern of the No Kings movement can be understood through a structuralist lens as a</p><p>system-level interaction between state action and protest. As shown in Table 1, national-level</p><p>government decisions that were widely interpreted as anti-democratic heightened the salience of</p><p>perceived executive overreach and triggered new waves of mobilization. The October 1, 2025</p><p>government shutdown and Operation Midway Blitz, which involved over 1,500</p><p>immigration-related arrests, preceded No Kings II (Doyle [ACLED], November 2025), while the</p><p>onset of the 2026 Iran War preceded No Kings III (Doyle [ACLED], April 2026).</p><p>These events didn’t act in isolation but interacted with existing grievances and</p><p>organizational capacity. By reinforcing the movement’s core framing of executive overreach,</p><p>they expanded the pool of potential participants and activated established networks. The result is</p><p>a reinforcing feedback process in which major government actions catalyze protest, and</p><p>subsequent mobilization increases the likelihood of further contention. This dynamic produces</p><p>non-linear escalation across successive waves.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 9\"><p>Figure 3. Structural causal loop diagram of the No Kings movement. This diagram</p><p>illustrates how pre-existing structural conditions enabled large-scale protest and how subsequent</p><p>state actions contributed to its escalation. Declining institutional trust and established</p><p>organizational infrastructure created a population primed for mobilization. National-level</p><p>government decisions perceived as anti-democratic increased the salience of executive</p><p>overreach, triggering initial protest waves. These waves then entered a reinforcing state-protest</p><p>feedback loop, in which state responses generated additional grievances, in turn expanding</p><p>participation and strengthening mobilization capacity.</p><p>III. Constructivist Analysis</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 10\"><p>Figure 4. ACLED monthly demonstration trends for the United States, May 2025-April</p><p>2026. Three sharp spikes (June, October, end of March) correspond to the three No Kings waves.</p><p>(Source: ACLED, 2025)</p><p>As shown in Figure 4, while protests occurred continuously over time, there are three</p><p>clear spikes in participation corresponding to the major No Kings waves. This pattern of</p><p>concentrated escalation cannot be explained by structural conditions alone. While structuralism</p><p>accounts for the material conditions that make protest possible, it doesn’t explain why many</p><p>first-time participants understood their actions as necessary within a specifically democratic,</p><p>anti-monarchical framework. Constructivist approaches instead emphasize that political behavior</p><p>is shaped by shared meanings, identities, and narratives that give actions their significance</p><p>(Finnemore &amp; Sikkink, 1998). In this context, the key question is what collective identity</p><p>participants adopted and what narrative made participation appear both necessary and legitimate.</p><p>Participant testimony shows that individuals framed protest as a civic and moral</p><p>obligation rather than a policy preference. One protester described participation as continuing a</p><p>“legacy for social justice…to carry that on in my generation,” while another stated that</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 11\"><p>“democracy is under threat” and that they wanted their “voice to be heard with other people”</p><p>(CNN, 2026). First-time participants expressed a similar shift from observation to obligation,</p><p>noting that “when you see things going wrong…it feels good to…speak up” (CNN, 2026). These</p><p>statements indicate that participation was interpreted as fulfilling a democratic role. Importantly,</p><p>this role was framed as consistent with defending foundational democratic principles, positioning</p><p>protest not as rejection of the political system, but as an effort to uphold its core ideals (CNN,</p><p>2026).</p><p>Figure 5-6. Photos of the No Kings protests and participant messaging (BBC, 2026;</p><p>NPR, 2026). These images illustrate both the scale of mass mobilization and the symbolic</p><p>framing that shaped participation. Large, densely packed crowds demonstrate the movement’s</p><p>capacity for nationwide turnout, while protest signs such as “No Kings” and pro-democracy</p><p>messaging reflect how participants interpreted their actions through an internalized democratic</p><p>identity that framed protest as a legitimate and necessary civic act.</p><p>The movement’s name, “No Kings,” is also central to this identity construction. It</p><p>operates as a symbolic frame that reinterprets contemporary political conflict through a</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 12\"><p>historically embedded narrative of resistance to concentrated power. By invoking the founding</p><p>rejection of monarchy, it collapses diverse grievances into a single, legible claim about</p><p>illegitimate authority (PBS, 2026). It gives participants a common framework for understanding</p><p>their actions as part of a broader democratic struggle. &quot;This framing lowers the threshold for</p><p>participation by simplifying complex issues into a clear moral distinction, linking present action</p><p>to familiar democratic traditions, and enabling rapid diffusion (PBS, 2026).</p><p>Elite responses further illustrate the importance of meaning construction. The White</p><p>House dismissed the protests as “Trump Derangement Therapy Sessions,” attempting to</p><p>delegitimize participants (BBC, 2026; CNN, 2026). However, continued mobilization suggests</p><p>this counter-narrative failed and instead reinforced the movement’s core framing of democratic</p><p>threat. This reflects norm entrenchment: by failing to delegitimize protest, the administration</p><p>inadvertently confirmed the movement&#x27;s framing that democratic norms were under threat,</p><p>sustaining participation beyond the initial base.</p><p>IV. Critical Assessment</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 13\"><p>Figure 7. ACLED top 10 single-day demonstration totals in the United States since 2020.</p><p>No Kings I–III rank among the largest single-day protest events recorded, exceeding other recent</p><p>mobilizations. This comparative scale demonstrates the movement’s exceptional reach while also</p><p>serving as the foundations to indicate a gap between protest magnitude and institutional</p><p>outcomes (ACLED, 2026).</p><p>The Carnegie Endowment for International Peace provides a comparative benchmark,</p><p>arguing that “mass protest alone is not enough to halt democratic erosion…it must be converted</p><p>into electoral outcomes or institutional constraints on the executive” (Brechenmacher &amp; Joshi,</p><p>2026). On this metric, the No Kings movement is substantial but incomplete. The scale was</p><p>enabled by a decentralized model that lowered barriers to participation across diverse regions.</p><p>However, the movement’s broad framing as a “national resistance to tyranny,” rather than around</p><p>specific policy demands, facilitated participation while limiting its capacity to produce targeted</p><p>policy change.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 14\"><p>From a systems perspective, this gap is reflected in the dynamics shown in Figure 3.</p><p>Reinforcing interactions between mobilization, grievances, and organizational capacity produced</p><p>escalating waves of protest, but, as indicated in Table 1, these have not translated into policy or</p><p>leadership change. Comparative cases show that success depends on electoral alignment, which</p><p>remains limited in the U.S. context due to highly partisan participation.2 Recent shifts toward</p><p>local advocacy and voter protection ahead of the 2026 midterms suggest a transition from</p><p>mobilization to organizing. The movement’s long-term impact will depend on whether this</p><p>participation can be converted into sustained electoral and policy outcomes.</p><p>V. Conclusion</p><p>The No Kings movement&#x27;s three escalating waves emerged from the convergence of</p><p>structural preconditions, declining institutional legitimacy, pre-existing organizational</p><p>infrastructure, and state-protest feedback dynamics and a constructed democratic identity that</p><p>made participation feel morally necessary across diverse groups. Its historical significance,</p><p>however, remains contingent. Unprecedented mobilization has not yet produced institutional</p><p>change, and its long-term impact depends on whether the organizational capacity built across</p><p>three waves can be converted into electoral outcomes in 2026 and beyond.</p><p>Appendix A. Comparative Outcomes of Mass Protest Movements</p><p>2 This is further explored in Appendix A.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 15\"><p>This table expands on the comparative framework referenced in Section IV, illustrating</p><p>how mass protest movements vary in their ability to produce institutional change. Consistent</p><p>with the Carnegie Endowment analysis, the key differentiating factor across cases is whether</p><p>protest mobilization is successfully linked to electoral strategies and coalition-building.</p><div class=\"essay-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td>Case</td><td>Scale of Protest</td><td>Political Context</td><td>Electoral Alignment</td><td>Outcome</td><td>Key Mechanism</td></tr><tr><td>United States (No Kings, 2025–202 6)</td><td>~20 million cumulative participants</td><td>Democratic system with high polarization and declining trust. Rallying against Trump.</td><td>Weak: highly partisan participation with limited cross-party support</td><td>No major policy or leadership change to date</td><td>Strong mobilization but limited conversion to electoral influence</td></tr><tr><td>Poland (2020–20 23 protests)</td><td>Large-scale nationwide protests (e.g., abortion rights movement)</td><td>Democratic backsliding with competitive elections</td><td>Strong: opposition parties coordinated and targeted key voter groups</td><td>Electoral turnover and policy shifts</td><td>Mobilization linked to voter turnout campaigns and opposition alignment</td></tr><tr><td>Brazil (2022 protests)</td><td>Mass protests against democratic erosion and coup threats</td><td>Competitive electoral system under strain</td><td>Strong: broad coalitions across ideological groups</td><td>Electoral defeat of incumbent leadership</td><td>Coalition-build ing and alignment with viable electoral alternative</td></tr><tr><td>Georgia (2023–20 24 protests)</td><td>Sustained mass protests in urban centers</td><td>Hybrid regime with increasing repression</td><td>Weak: fragmented opposition and limited electoral coordination</td><td>No significant institutional change</td><td>Failure to translate protest into electoral leverage</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 16\"><div class=\"essay-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td>South Korea (2024–20 25 protests)</td><td>Large-scale protests following executive overreach (martial law attempt)</td><td>Democratic system with lower polarization on core issue</td><td>Strong: broad cross-partisan public support</td><td>Impeachment of president</td><td>High public consensus enabling institutional response</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Analytical Summary</p><p>Across cases, protest scale alone does not determine outcomes. Instead, institutional</p><p>impact depends on whether mobilization shifts the balance of political power through electoral</p><p>alignment. Cases such as Poland, Brazil, and South Korea demonstrate that when protest</p><p>movements build broad coalitions and connect to viable electoral alternatives, they can produce</p><p>policy or leadership change. In contrast, cases such as Georgia and the United States illustrate</p><p>that even large-scale mobilization may fail to generate institutional outcomes when participation</p><p>remains fragmented or highly partisan.</p><p>This comparison supports the argument in Section IV: the No Kings movement has</p><p>achieved exceptional mobilization, but its long-term impact will depend on whether it can</p><p>transition from protest to sustained electoral and organizational engagement.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 17\"><p>Appendix B. AI Statement</p><p>This assignment was done with the assistance of Claude (Anthropic) as a research and</p><p>drafting support tool, in accordance with Minerva’s AI use policy. AI was used to help locate and</p><p>synthesize relevant sources, including ACLED, Carnegie, and WVS materials, and to support the</p><p>organization and structuring of the argument. All analytical claims, interpretations, and final</p><p>written content are my own, and all sources were verified against original materials (all sources</p><p>were found by me, AI was used to help analyze and store those sources). Spelling and grammar</p><p>were reviewed using Grammarly, and citations were formatted using MyBib.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 18\"><p>Appendix C: LO and HC Justifications</p><p>Table C1. LOs Addressed</p><div class=\"essay-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td>LO</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td>#poliscimethods</td><td>The paper combines quantitative event data (ACLED protest counts, geographic distribution, violence classification rates) with qualitative sources (CNN testimony, Carnegie comparative analysis, PBS historical context) in an explicitly mixed-methods case study design. The causal loop diagram (Figure 3) constitutes an original visualization translating ACLED data into a theoretical model. The WVS Wave 7 survey data (attitudinal frequencies on institutional confidence) are cited as empirical baselines. This methodological pluralism is itself named and justified in the analysis: structuralism requires material/quantitative indicators, constructivism requires interpretive/qualitative evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>#casestudy</td><td>The paper uses the United States (2025–2026) as a bounded historical case study, structured around a clear research question (why did No Kings emerge in three escalating waves and how did it scale?). The Carnegie Endowment&#x27;s comparative framework, drawing on South Korea, Poland, Brazil, Georgia, and Serbia, is used to benchmark the U.S. case against analogous instances of mass mobilization under democratic backsliding. This structured comparison isolates the electoral-conversion variable as the key differentiator between movements that achieved institutional impact and those that did not, following the logic of comparative case analysis. The timeline table (Table 1) explicitly operationalizes the case&#x27;s temporal boundaries and key events.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 19\"><div class=\"essay-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td>LO</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td>#ss112-constructivism</td><td>Section III applies constructivism to explain why structural conditions alone cannot account for the movement&#x27;s specific form. Protester testimony (CNN, 2026) is analyzed as evidence of intersubjective meaning-making: participants framed protest as civic obligation rather than policy preference. The movement&#x27;s name is interpreted as a symbolic frame invoking the founding rejection of monarchy, collapsing diverse grievances into a single legible claim. The White House counter-narrative is analyzed through norm entrenchment: its failure to suppress mobilization demonstrates that protest had become widely understood as legitimate civic action.</td></tr><tr><td>#ss112-integratingtheories</td><td>The paper explicitly integrates structuralism and constructivism as distinct but complementary lenses applied to the same empirical phenomenon. The integration is methodologically justified: structuralism explains the necessary preconditions (legitimacy erosion, organizational capacity, feedback loops) that made large-scale protest possible, while constructivism explains the specific ideational form the movement took (democratic identity frame, anti-monarchical narrative) and why that form achieved resonance across diverse groups. Section IV synthesizes both: the structural analysis explains the three-wave escalation pattern, the constructivist analysis explains sustained participation, and the critical assessment evaluates both against the Carnegie comparative benchmark for institutional impact.</td></tr><tr><td>#ss112-structuralism</td><td>Section II identifies three structural mechanisms driving the movement: (1) legitimacy erosion as a standing condition, evidenced by WVS Wave 7 data showing roughly</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 20\"><div class=\"essay-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td>LO</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>three-quarters of U.S. respondents lacked confidence in the federal government; (2) pre-existing organizational infrastructure, evidenced by the coalition of 200+ organizations (Indivisible, 50501, MoveOn, ACLU, AFT) that had already demonstrated operational capacity through ~500 Hands Off protests in April 2025; and (3) a reinforcing state-protest feedback loop in which major government actions (the government shutdown, Operation Midway Blitz, the Iran War) catalyzed successive protest waves. The causal loop diagram (Figure 3) visualizes these mechanisms explicitly.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Table C2. HC Addressed</p><div class=\"essay-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td>HC</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td>#systemmapping</td><td>Section II identifies a reinforcing feedback loop as the structural driver of the movement’s escalation. State actions such as ICE raids, National Guard deployments, the government shutdown, and the Iran War increased grievances, which expanded the pool of potential participants, strengthened organizational capacity, and amplified subsequent protest waves. This dynamic explains the non-linear growth from approximately 4-6 million participants in No Kings I (1,600+ events), to around 7 million in No Kings II (1,950+ events), and roughly 8 million in No Kings III (2,400+ events), despite no significant change in organizational strategy.</td></tr><tr><td>#complexcausality</td><td>The paper&#x27;s central argument is explicitly multi-causal. Section I distinguishes structural preconditions (declining institutional trust documented in WVS Wave 7, organizational infrastructure in place by April 2025) from proximate triggering events (the June 14, 2025</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 21\"><div class=\"essay-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td>HC</td><td>Application</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>military parade, ICE raids). Section III adds a third causal layer: the constructed democratic identity frame that made participation feel morally necessary. Neither structural conditions, nor triggering events, nor framing alone explains the movement; all three are necessary.</td></tr><tr><td>#emergentproperties</td><td>No Kings emerged from the decentralized interaction of 200+ civil society organizations, and pre-existing civic infrastructure. The result; 8 million participants across 3,300 sites in 50 states by March 28th, 2026; was not planned or directed by any single actor but arose from distributed coordination. No single organization could have achieved this scale independently. The movement&#x27;s geographic reach into traditionally conservative states (Wyoming, South Dakota, Alabama) and its (later) extension to 15 countries were emergent outcomes of the decentralized model, not intended design features.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 22\"><p>Appendix D. Bibliography</p><p>Asperin, A. M. (2026, March 26). No Kings 2026: Full list of protest locations, times across</p><p>Southern California. FOX 11 Los Angeles.</p><p>https://www.foxla.com/news/no-kings-day-protest-march-28-california-locations</p><p>Doyle, K. (2025a, August 8). United States and Canada Overview: August 2025. ACLED.</p><p>https://acleddata.com/update/united-states-and-canada-overview-august-2025</p><p>Doyle, K. (2025b, October 16). The first No Kings protests were massive. Will Saturday’s No</p><p>Kings II reach the same heights? — Expert comment. ACLED.</p><p>https://acleddata.com/expert-comment/first-no-kings-protests-were-massive-will-saturday</p><p>s-no-kings-ii-reach-same-heights</p><p>Doyle, K. (2025c, November 6). United States and Canada Overview: November 2025. ACLED.</p><p>https://acleddata.com/update/united-states-and-canada-overview-november-2025</p><p>Doyle, K. (2026a, January 12). United States and Canada Overview: January 2026. ACLED.</p><p>https://acleddata.com/update/united-states-and-canada-overview-january-2026</p><p>Doyle, K. (2026b, April 9). United States and Canada Overview: April 2026. ACLED.</p><p>https://acleddata.com/update/united-states-and-canada-overview-april-2026</p><p>Finnemore, M., &amp; Sikkink, K. (1998). International Norm Dynamics and Political Change.</p><p>International Organization, 52(4), 887–917.</p><p>https://library.fes.de/libalt/journals/swetsfulltext/5333873.pdf</p><p>Global Protest Tracker. (2017). Carnegie Endowment for International Peace.</p><p>https://carnegieendowment.org/features/global-protest-tracker</p><p>Ramanathan, T. (2025, June 24). No Kings protests | Meaning, Turnout, Numbers, &amp; Around the</p><p>World. Encyclopedia Britannica. https://www.britannica.com/event/No-Kings-protests</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 23\"><p>Saskia Brechenmacher, &amp; Joshi, S. (2026, April 3). Protests Like No Kings Can Only Go So Far</p><p>to Stem Authoritarianism. Carnegie Endowment for International Peace.</p><p>https://carnegieendowment.org/emissary/2026/04/no-kings-protest-rally-democracy-strate</p><p>gy</p><p>Sottile, Z. (2026, March 30). Why new and seasoned protesters alike say they came out to “No</p><p>Kings” rallies across the country. CNN.</p><p>https://edition.cnn.com/2026/03/30/us/no-kings-protests-why-people-joined</p><p>Venkatraman, S. (2026, March 28). No Kings protests: Thousands across the US rally against</p><p>Trump. BBC News. https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cq8wy7g1gd1o</p><p>W Phillips Shively. (2022). POWER AND CHOICE : an introduction to political science.</p><p>Rowman &amp; Littlefield.</p><p>https://www.bloomsbury.com/us/power-and-choice-9781538151860/</p><p>Wikipedia Contributors. (2025a, June 15). No Kings protests. Wikipedia; Wikimedia Foundation.</p><p>https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No_Kings_protests</p><p>Wikipedia Contributors. (2025b, October 27). June 2025 No Kings protests. Wikipedia;</p><p>Wikimedia Foundation. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/June_2025_No_Kings_protests</p><p>Woodruff, J., &amp; Lane, S. (2026, April 2). How “No Kings” rallies fit into America’s history of</p><p>protest. PBS News.</p><p>https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/how-no-kings-rallies-fit-into-americas-history-of-pr</p><p>otest</p><p>World Values Survey. (2022). World Values Survey Wave 7 (2017-2022).</p><p>Www.worldvaluessurvey.org.</p><p>https://www.worldvaluessurvey.org/WVSDocumentationWV7.jsp</p></section>"}, {"id": "lens", "title": "Writing Journal 1", "date": "", "tag": "WRITING", "excerpt": "Full text from Writing Journal 1.pdf", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"writing/lens.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read the original PDF ↗</a> · Full text, including references and assignment notes. Figures are available in the PDF.</p><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 1\"><p>WRITING JOURNAL 1</p><p>Journal Entry 1</p><p>Prompt: After this class, write an entry about your own personal experience of #interpretivelens.</p><p>Explain how you became aware of your own #interpretivelens in interpreting a communicative</p><p>text (defined loosely here - can be a literary, journalistic, visual, aural, or multimedia text).</p><p>Date: Wednesday, September 11 | Time Intended: 1 hour | Time Spent: 1 hour | HC:</p><p>#interpretivelens</p><p>Purpose for Writing:</p><p>I want to use this opportunity not just to complete an assignment but to reflect deeply on</p><p>the texts I&#x27;ve read over the past few years. While I frequently discuss how a handful of books</p><p>have influenced my perspectives, I haven&#x27;t had the chance to truly analyze how and why these</p><p>changes occurred. Additionally, I see this as a chance to hone in my narrative writing skills.</p><p>Although I&#x27;ve written many articles, newsletters, and analytical essays in school, I haven&#x27;t had</p><p>much practice with creative or narrative writing where I can express my emotions and thoughts</p><p>more freely. As I began working on personal statements and essays for college applications, I</p><p>realized how out of practice I am in this area.</p><p>“Our first book on the reading list is The Book of Trespas by Nick Hayes,” my economics</p><p>teacher declared with a dramatic flourish, holding up the book for everyone to see. The cover</p><p>was a vivid splash of bold red lines: a deer standing apprehensively against a backdrop of tangled</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 2\"><p>barbed wire. The image was jarring, a far cry from the mundane school textbooks I was used to. I</p><p>couldn’t help but think, “This is stupid.” The book seemed like a bizarre choice, completely out</p><p>of sync with the rigid, numbers-driven world of economics I was expecting.</p><p>When Arvin (not Mr. Dang or Professor Dang) handed me The Book of Trespass, I rolled</p><p>my eyes. I had always seen him as an oddity—a former Wall Street economist turned Sherpa</p><p>turned teacher. Growing up in a conservative, predominantly white town before moving to a</p><p>liberal international boarding school, I was accustomed to a specific view of what education</p><p>should be. To me, my teacher’s suggestion felt like a misguided detour from the conventional,</p><p>grades-focused education I was used to. I thought of him as a “hippie” with questionable ideas</p><p>and had little interest in what seemed like an irrelevant book.</p><p>With a sense of obligation rather than curiosity, I started reading The Book of Trespass.</p><p>My initial approach was dismissive; I skimmed through the pages, eager to find evidence that it</p><p>was a waste of time. My focus was on finishing the book quickly so I could return to my “real”</p><p>studies. But as I continued reading, something unexpected happened. Hayes’ storytelling</p><p>surrounding the dynamics of ownership and access began to catch my interest. His examination</p><p>of how property and space are tied to social inequality and historical injustices opened my eyes</p><p>in ways I hadn’t anticipated.</p><p>The deeper I got into the book, especially the chapter on women’s rights and historical</p><p>power dynamics (Spider), I realized how much my initial judgment was clouded by bias. Hayes</p><p>wasn’t just discussing land laws; he was unravelling a complex history of exclusion and control</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 3\"><p>that resonated far beyond economics. For example, in &quot;Spider,&quot; Hayes explores how women</p><p>were systematically marginalized, from witch hunts to their exclusion based on the power they</p><p>wielded through gossip at the commons. Chapters like this led me to view the text through a new</p><p>lens, prompting me to question my own assumptions about education and learning. The book</p><p>was forcing me to confront my own narrow view of what was valuable in an academic context.</p><p>This revelation was a turning point for me. I understood that my initial resistance was a</p><p>result of my own limited perspective. I had judged my teacher and his recommendations based</p><p>on my own preconceived notions, rather than engaging with the material openly. The Book of</p><p>Trespass wasn’t a detour from my academic goals; it was a profound lesson in how important it</p><p>is to be open to new ideas and perspectives. By the time I finished the book, my view of learning</p><p>had shifted dramatically. I realized that education isn’t just about following a prescribed path to</p><p>achieve good grades. It’s about exploring diverse viewpoints, challenging my own assumptions,</p><p>and embracing ideas that initially seem foreign or irrelevant. This experience taught me that</p><p>sometimes the most valuable lessons come from unexpected places and that being willing to</p><p>question and expand my interpretive lens can lead to profound personal growth.1</p><p>1 In applying #responsibility, I dedicated time to this entry and faced the challenge of writing narratively (the style I chose to practice). By revising my work and analyzing the narrative style of the text, I immersed myself in the moment my narrative is based on while reflecting on it in hindsight. This approach helped me overcome the obstacles and produce a thoughtful entry.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 4\"><p>Journal Entry 2</p><p>Prompt: Write in your journal something from the Chiang short story you noticed through close</p><p>reading (not discussed in class) and write up a short #critique.</p><p>Date: Wednesday, September 18 | Time Intended: 1 hour | Time Spent: 1 hour 45 minutes | HC:</p><p>#critique</p><p>Purpose for Writing:</p><p>Several points of investigation intrigued me. For instance, I was fascinated by how the</p><p>story begins and ends with the same statement (similar to a book I enjoy, The Outsiders), which</p><p>highlighted Louise&#x27;s experience of living through all time—past, present, and</p><p>future—simultaneously. This led me to further develop the themes of free will vs determinism.</p><p>Since we didn&#x27;t have the opportunity to explore these topics extensively in class, I used this space</p><p>to discuss their connections and investigate how they add significant depth to the narrative.</p><p>Ted Chiang’s short story Story of Your Life talks about complex themes of time,</p><p>language, free will, and determinism, inviting readers into a narrative that reflects both</p><p>philosophical and linguistic inquiries. Through a close reading, I observed how Chiang uses</p><p>narrative structure, tense manipulation, and recurring motifs to deepen the readers&#x27; understanding</p><p>of these themes—particularly in ways that weren’t discussed in class. One aspect that stands out</p><p>through a more focused analysis is how Chiang frames the narrative with a recurring proposition</p><p>and how he uses the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis as a lens to explore the human experience of time.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 5\"><p>Together, these elements provide a nuanced exploration of the tension between free will and</p><p>determinism, ultimately suggesting that our lives, despite being constrained by certain</p><p>inevitabilities, are imbued with meaning through the choices we make.</p><p>As we touched on in class, Chiang draws heavily on the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis, which</p><p>suggests that language shapes thought and influences how individuals perceive reality. Chiang</p><p>uses this concept to explore the way Louise Banks’ mastery of Heptapod B transforms her</p><p>perception of time. What stands out here is that Chiang doesn&#x27;t treat this idea as purely</p><p>theoretical; instead, he immerses readers in Louise&#x27;s evolving comprehension of time through his</p><p>manipulation of tenses and narrative structure. What we didn’t fully explore in class is how</p><p>Chiang extends this idea beyond language itself by framing the entire story in a way that echoes</p><p>this lack of linearity (specifically with its opening and closing statements).</p><p>The story’s structural framing—marked by the recurring question, “Do you want to make</p><p>a baby?”—provides another layer of meaning related to time, choice, and acceptance. At first,</p><p>the question seems to reflect a personal decision between Louise and her partner, but as her</p><p>understanding of time expands, it takes on a broader existential significance. The repetition of</p><p>this proposition at both the beginning and the end of the narrative emphasizes the simultaneous</p><p>yet cyclical nature of time, mirroring the Heptapods’ non-linear worldview. Just as their</p><p>language allows them to perceive all events simultaneously rather than sequentially, the circular</p><p>structure of the story reflects this specific perception of reality. At the beginning of the story, the</p><p>framing device may seem confusing and irrelevant, but by the time you finish and connect the</p><p>narrative&#x27;s dots, it takes on much deeper significance. By the end of the story, it’s clear that the</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 6\"><p>question, “Do you want to make a baby?” is more than a query about parenthood—it is a</p><p>meditation on fate, choice, and the acceptance of life’s inevitable pain and joy.2</p><p>Louise’s foreknowledge of her daughter’s short life could be seen as an argument for</p><p>determinism—her future is already set, and there’s nothing she can do to change it. But what’s</p><p>striking about the story is that Louise’s foreknowledge does not lead her to resist her fate or</p><p>attempt to change the outcome. Instead, she embraces it fully, choosing to live within the</p><p>moments of joy and sorrow as they unfold. This is where Chiang’s portrayal of free will diverges</p><p>from a traditional understanding. Rather than seeing foreknowledge as a form of entrapment,</p><p>Louise’s acceptance of her future represents an active, intentional engagement with her life.</p><p>Knowing what’s to come doesn’t diminish the value of her experiences; if anything, it enhances</p><p>them by giving her the opportunity to appreciate each moment more deeply. In this sense,</p><p>Chiang’s story offers a rethinking of what it means to exercise free will. Rather than focusing on</p><p>the ability to change the future, the story suggests that true agency lies in how we choose to</p><p>engage with the inevitabilities of life.</p><p>In the end, Chiang’s story is not just about language, time, or determinism—it is a</p><p>meditation on the nature of human existence. The Sapir-Whorf hypothesis, the cyclical narrative</p><p>structure, and the tension between free will and determinism all converge to explore what it</p><p>means to live fully in the face of inevitable loss. As my time at UWC, the high school that held</p><p>some of the best years of my life, drew to a close, I felt the inevitable sadness of leaving behind a</p><p>2 Here, the #interpretivelens is used to analyze how the cyclical nature of the story alters the reader&#x27;s interpretation. By framing the narrative this way, the author demonstrates how language shapes perception, both through Louise’s experiences and in a meta sense by influencing the reader&#x27;s understanding of the story&#x27;s format.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 7\"><p>place filled with so many memories. But something my English teacher often reminded us of was</p><p>the ephemerality of these experiences—that their fleeting nature is precisely what makes them so</p><p>special. Just as Louise’s journey raises the profound question: if we knew the future, would we</p><p>live our lives any differently? Her answer is no—her knowledge of what’s to come doesn’t lessen</p><p>the value of her experiences; it deepens her appreciation for life’s fleeting moments. Similarly,</p><p>knowing my time at UWC was temporary didn&#x27;t make it any less meaningful. In fact, it</p><p>heightened my sense of gratitude for every moment I spent there.3</p><p>Knowing that something will end doesn’t take away its significance; it makes it all the</p><p>more precious. Both in Chiang’s story and in my own life, the fleeting nature of time is what</p><p>drives us to cherish the present and live fully, even in the face of inevitable goodbyes.4</p><p>3 I use my #context as a UWC student to shape my #interpretivelens when reading the book. My experiences at UWC, allow me to connect deeply with Louise&#x27;s journey. This personal context enriches my interpretation, highlighting how awareness of ephemerality fosters appreciation for the present.</p><p>4 In applying #responsibility, I used my annotations to identify intriguing points for investigation that weren’t discussed in class. An obstacle I faced was exploring not just the &quot;what&quot; of the narrative but also the &quot;how&quot; Chiang used textual features to examine determinism and its significance. I overcame this by connecting my writing to my own #interpretivelens, analyzing my perspective alongside those of Louise and Chiang.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 8\"><p>Journal Entry 3</p><p>Prompt: Write an HC footnote justification for #critique for your journal entry 2.</p><p>Date: Thursday, September 26 | Time Intended: 20 minutes | Time Spent: 15 minutes | HC:</p><p>#critique</p><p>Purpose for Writing:</p><p>Practicing how to write HC footnote justifications, is a useful skill that will be used in</p><p>many or all of our assignments to effectively signpost our intentions in writing and demonstrate</p><p>fulfillment of the HC rubric. Through this, I aim to develop clarity in articulating how my</p><p>analysis and interpretive choices align with the criteria and purpose of higher-level critique.</p><p>In applying #critique, I examined how Chiang’s narrative structure and the Sapir-Whorf</p><p>hypothesis deepen the thematic exploration of determinism and free will in Story of Your Life. A</p><p>challenge I encountered was moving beyond the discussion of these ideas in class to analyze how</p><p>Chiang’s manipulation of time and language specifically contributes to the reader’s</p><p>understanding of these themes. I addressed this by engaging with the text through a more focused</p><p>#interpretivelens, exploring how the cyclical structure and recurring motifs reinforce the</p><p>non-linear perception of time, offering a more nuanced critique of fate and human agency.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 9\"><p>Journal Entry 4</p><p>Prompt: Describe an issue you care about that you think is worthy of a strong and clear #thesis to</p><p>convince others on this issue.</p><p>Date: Thursday, October 3 | Time Intended: 20 minutes | Time Spent: 15 minutes | HC: #thesis</p><p>Purpose for Writing:</p><p>The purpose of this writing is to connect my passion for addressing the harmful effects of</p><p>fast fashion with a strong argumentative thesis. By examining the environmental damage and</p><p>worker exploitation linked to fast fashion, I aim to develop a compelling thesis that highlights the</p><p>urgency of this issue. Using examples like Shein, I want to inspire readers to support</p><p>eco-friendly brands and sustainable practices while refining my skills in crafting persuasive</p><p>arguments on topics I care about.</p><p>An issue I care about is the harmful impact of fast fashion on both the environment and</p><p>workers in developing countries. The fast fashion industry generates millions of tons of textile</p><p>waste each year, contributes to 20% of global wastewater, and is responsible for 10% of</p><p>worldwide carbon emissions. Moreover, it often relies on low-wage labour, exploiting workers</p><p>under poor conditions. Brands like Shein epitomize this problem, prioritizing quantity over</p><p>quality, fueling a culture of micro-trends, wasting vast amounts of fabric, and being notorious for</p><p>child labour and sweatshops with shockingly low wages. A compelling argument could highlight</p><p>how fast fashion’s unsustainable practices not only damage the planet but also violate human</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 10\"><p>rights. It would encourage consumers to support ethical alternatives by choosing eco-friendly</p><p>brands, buying secondhand, and reducing overconsumption. It is essential to educate oneself on</p><p>how micro-trends fuel the fast fashion industry and to actively avoid brands that engage in</p><p>exploitative labour practices. Additionally, promoting upcycling, learning to mend, and repairing</p><p>clothes—such as fixing small holes or missing buttons—can prevent perfectly wearable items</p><p>from being discarded. Convincing people to move away from fast fashion is vital for fostering</p><p>environmental sustainability and ensuring fair labour practices.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 11\"><p>Journal Entry 5</p><p>Prompt: Write in your journal what you thought was the most compelling piece of evidence on</p><p>identity in this unit so far (From one of these authors: Love, Pavia, Galston, The New York</p><p>Times video, or Appiah). Write an HC footnote for #evidencebased as though you used this</p><p>evidence and this was your essay for which you will receive HC scores.</p><p>Date: Thursday, October 14th | Time Intended: 30 minutes | Time Spent: 1 hour| HC:</p><p>#evidencebased</p><p>Purpose for Writing:</p><p>In this journal entry, I used the opportunity to further dissect Appiah&#x27;s writing and explore</p><p>how he makes his work compelling by diversifying the types of evidence he employs to support</p><p>his arguments. I look at the impacts of each type of evidence he uses - such as the appeal to</p><p>pathos in personal anecdotes - and the appeal to logos/ethos in intellectual history and case</p><p>studies. I also use this entry to practice using evidence in essay writing through the HC footnote.</p><p>In my reflection on the unit, I found Kwame Anthony Appiah&#x27;s discussion on identity in</p><p>The Lies That Bind the most compelling, especially the persuasive rhetoric around how identities</p><p>are formed and contested. One point that stood out was his argument that we often mistakenly</p><p>assume there is some deep essence at the core of identities that unites individuals (Appiah,</p><p>2018). This notion reflects essentialism, where we believe a hidden, unobservable trait defines a</p><p>group. He effectively deconstructs various concepts of identity by utilizing a diverse range of</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 12\"><p>evidence—such as personal anecdotes, intellectual history, and case studies—crafting a</p><p>comprehensive narrative that makes his work the most compelling piece of literature we&#x27;ve</p><p>studied in this class.</p><p>Through personal anecdotes, Appiah recounts his own experience of being mistaken for</p><p>different ethnicities in various countries. In one instance, a London taxi driver refused to believe</p><p>that Appiah didn’t speak Hindi, illustrating how identity is often judged based on external</p><p>appearances and cultural assumptions rather than any inherent, deep-rooted essence. In exploring</p><p>intellectual history, Appiah investigates how modern conceptions of identity, particularly racial</p><p>categories, were shaped during the 19th century. He critiques early scientific theories that sought</p><p>to classify people based on superficial traits like skin colour, highlighting how these outdated</p><p>ideas continue to influence contemporary understandings of identity. Lastly, Appiah draws on</p><p>case studies, such as the Robbers Cave experiment, where boys quickly formed group identities</p><p>based on arbitrary divisions. This study demonstrates how identities can be socially constructed</p><p>and manipulated, reinforcing Appiah’s argument that identity is fluid rather than fixed.</p><p>These different types of evidence not only build a more comprehensive and concrete</p><p>argument but also enhance the variety of persuasive techniques he uses. Through personal</p><p>anecdotes, Appiah appeals to pathos, drawing readers into a more emotional and immersive</p><p>connection with the text. Meanwhile, his use of intellectual history and case studies appeals to</p><p>both logos and ethos, providing logical reasoning and credible research to support his claims.</p><p>Overall, the diverse range of evidence and persuasive strategies Appiah employs makes his text</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 13\"><p>the most compelling in its ability to challenge essentialist views on identity while engaging</p><p>readers on both emotional and intellectual levels.</p><p>HC Footnote:</p><p>This essay demonstrates a strong application of #evidencebased by highlighting key</p><p>evidence, such as Appiah’s personal anecdotes and critiques of intellectual history to reinforce</p><p>the thesis that his diverse use of evidence enhances the overall persuasiveness of his writing</p><p>(makes it the most compelling). To support my claims, I analyze how Appiah’s various types of</p><p>evidence influence reader persuasion, explaining how they appeal to the three persuasive</p><p>appeals: pathos, logos, and ethos. Additionally, I arrange this evidence effectively, which is</p><p>essential to #evidencebased writing. The essay begins with an introduction and thesis statement,</p><p>followed by a logical presentation of evidence that anticipates the audience&#x27;s need for evidentiary</p><p>support. I address this using the structure of my body paragraphs; a clear claim, then paraphrased</p><p>examples from the text, and an analysis of the underlying implications of Appiah&#x27;s persuasive</p><p>techniques (what, how, and so what, as discussed in class).5</p><p>5 In completing this writing journal assignment, I demonstrated #responsibility by consistently allocating time for each entry, focusing on improving my writing skills. I wrote my reflections after each session, as recommended, to maintain relevance and clarity rather than waiting until the end and risking procrastination. Despite a busy schedule filled with other assignments and courses, I set aside specific time slots in my calendar for journaling and tried my best to adhere to the time intended. I also faced challenges in understanding some prompts, which I addressed by seeking clarification from my professor and consulting friends/peer tutors for guidance on how to approach the #evidencebased HC footnote.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 14\"><p>AI REFLECTION</p><p>I followed the assignment instructions carefully and did not use any AI tools for this task, as</p><p>requested. My goal was to challenge myself and improve my own writing skills without relying</p><p>on AI-generated content. However, I did use Grammarly and the built-in spell checker in Google</p><p>Docs to ensure proper spelling and grammar. These tools helped me catch any basic errors, but</p><p>all of the ideas and writing are entirely my own.</p></section>"}, {"id": "colonial-legacy", "title": "Beneath the Maple Leaf: Unmasking Canada’s Colonial Legacy and Indigenous Realities Within its National Identity", "date": "November 16, 2024", "tag": "WRITING", "excerpt": "Full text from THESIS-DRIVEN ESSAY (FINAL).pdf", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"writing/colonial-legacy.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read the original PDF ↗</a> · Full text, including references and assignment notes. Figures are available in the PDF.</p><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 1\"><p>THESIS-DRIVEN ESSAY</p><p>Beneath the Maple Leaf: Unmasking Canada&#x27;s Colonial Legacy and Indigenous</p><p>Realities Within its National Identity</p><p>Minerva University</p><p>MC50 Multimodal Communications</p><p>Professor M. Bogucki</p><p>November 16, 2024</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 2\"><p>Canada&#x27;s image of harmony and inclusivity conceals a darker reality: beneath the surface</p><p>of multicultural pride lies a legacy of colonialism and the ongoing marginalization of Indigenous</p><p>peoples. The country’s reputation as a welcoming, tolerant, and progressive society is central to</p><p>its national identity, drawing admiration both within and beyond its borders. This narrative is</p><p>reinforced by symbols of inclusion, such as multicultural festivals and Canada’s Charter of</p><p>Rights and Freedoms (Government of Canada, 2019), which enshrine principles of equality and</p><p>non-discrimination.1 However, this idealized version of Canada presents an incomplete picture,</p><p>disregarding a long history of colonial policies and practices that sought to erase Indigenous</p><p>cultures, languages, and sovereignty. Moreover, the consequences of these policies are not relics</p><p>of the past; the ongoing struggles for land rights, the enduring legacy of residential schools, and</p><p>the persistent failure to address systemic issues like intergenerational trauma and discrimination</p><p>reveal contradictions within the carefully polished image that Canada wishes to sustain. This</p><p>paper seeks to challenge the misrepresentation of Canada’s national identity by looking into the</p><p>historical and continuing impacts of colonialism on Indigenous communities. In doing so, it aims</p><p>to uncover the complex realities of Canadian nationalism and expose the disparities between its</p><p>professed values of inclusivity and the lived experiences of its First Nations peoples.2</p><p>The romanticized portrayal of Canada as a multicultural society often conceals the</p><p>colonial foundations upon which the country was built. While the ideal of multiculturalism</p><p>1 #context: This is a strong application of #context because it provides background to the widely accepted narrative of Canada’s “harmonious” public image. By grounding this perception with the Charter of Rights and Freedoms (Government of Canada, 2019), these statements lay a foundation for understanding how this image has been constructed. This framing is crucial for later dissecting the mischaracterization, as it allows for a meaningful contrast between Canada’s public image and the often-overlooked realities of Indigenous marginalization.</p><p>2 #thesis: #thesis is well used here because it establishes the paper’s central argument: challenging the misrepresentation of Canada’s national identity by examining the impacts of colonialism on Indigenous communities. By framing the thesis around the contrast between Canada’s professed values of inclusivity and the lived experiences of First Nations peoples, the statement sets up a critical exploration of Canadian nationalism.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 3\"><p>gained prominence in the mid-20th century as a response to increasing immigration, it was</p><p>largely designed without meaningful inclusion of Indigenous perspectives, as Wayland (1997)</p><p>highlights. Multiculturalism sought to assimilate newcomers into a unified Canadian identity, yet</p><p>it ignored the distinct status of Indigenous peoples as the original stewards of the land. This</p><p>exclusion shows the colonial mindset that continues to shape Canadian policy and societal</p><p>norms, relegating Indigenous peoples to the role of secondary citizens within their own</p><p>territories. This perspective of Indigenous communities as an afterthought is exemplified by the</p><p>widespread dispossession of their lands through unfair treaties and outright land confiscation.</p><p>Treaties were often negotiated under circumstances of unequal power. Indigenous leaders were</p><p>misled, pressured, or forced into agreements that stripped them of territories in exchange for</p><p>promises of protection, resources, or compensation that were rarely fulfilled. For example, the</p><p>numbered treaties, which cover much of Canada’s western provinces, were presented as mutual</p><p>agreements but often included language that Indigenous signatories either could not understand</p><p>or were assured would not harm their sovereignty (Hall, 2011). Beyond the treaties, land</p><p>confiscation without consent further exemplified the systemic disregard for Indigenous rights.</p><p>Government policies like the Indian Act restricted Indigenous control over their territories,</p><p>making it easier for settlers to exploit these lands (Parrott, 2006). Even today, legal battles over</p><p>unceded territories, such as those in British Columbia, highlight the long-standing inequities.</p><p>These disputes over land rights reveal the persistence of colonial structures, with Indigenous</p><p>peoples enduring systemic exploitation and unfilled commitments.3 The challenges they face</p><p>3 #organization: This is a strong application of #organization because the paragraph is structured effectively to support the paper’s central argument. It starts with a clear topic sentence directly tied to the #thesis. Evidence is then presented to suppport the claims, followed by a detailed unpacking of the evidence to provide depth and explanation. Then, the concluding sentence does not just summarize but synthesizes the argument, drawing broader implications that transition into the next paragraph.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 4\"><p>highlight Canada’s failure to honour promises made in treaties, reflecting a national identity still</p><p>deeply rooted in colonial injustice.</p><p>While the colonial history regarding land rights sets a critical foundation, the lasting</p><p>impact of its recent colonial practices, residential schools, exposes what this &quot;harmonious</p><p>national identity&quot; seeks to conceal. These institutions, which operated well into the late 20th</p><p>century with the last school closing in 1996, show a direct and systemic attempt to assimilate</p><p>Indigenous peoples. As Srikanth (2012) notes, residential schools forcibly removed Indigenous</p><p>children from their families, subjecting them to cultural erasure, physical abuse, and neglect. The</p><p>trauma inflicted by these schools has left deep scars within Indigenous communities, creating a</p><p>legacy of intergenerational pain and loss (Miller, 2012). Recent discoveries of unmarked graves</p><p>at former residential school sites have brought these atrocities into sharper public focus, serving</p><p>as a chilling reminder of the scale of this systemic violence. Shocking new evidence—such as</p><p>the discovery of over 200 graves at a single site, with investigations covering only a fraction of</p><p>known locations—has exposed the widespread nature of these abuses (Austen &amp; Bracken, 2024).</p><p>The graves are reminders of the lives lost and the families irreparably broken. Despite this, the</p><p>government’s limited efforts to investigate these findings, alongside the media’s tendency to</p><p>downplay their significance, reflect a broader reluctance to fully confront Canada’s colonial</p><p>legacy (Austen &amp; Bracken, 2022).4 This avoidance sustains a sanitized image of Canada that</p><p>prioritizes its reputation as a harmonious nation over addressing the lived realities of Indigenous</p><p>4 #evidencebased: This is a good example of #evidencebased because the paragraph is well-supported by multiple credible sources that substantiate its claims about the impact of residential schools. By citing Srikanth (2012), Miller (2012), and Austen &amp; Bracken (2024), the paragraph draws on diverse evidence, including historical analysis, documented trauma, and contemporary findings such as unmarked graves. This use of evidence not only illustrates the systemic nature of these injustices but also ties them to ongoing patterns of denial and avoidance.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 5\"><p>peoples. By refusing to confront its colonial past, with injustices such as unmarked graves being</p><p>sidelined, Canada perpetuates a national identity rooted in denial.</p><p>Regarding the disregard for injustice, in the aftermath of residential school closures,</p><p>Canada introduced reconciliation efforts, such as the establishment of the National Day for Truth</p><p>and Reconciliation (Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Canada, 2015). However, despite</p><p>these symbolic gestures and the country’s stated commitment to reconciliation with Indigenous</p><p>peoples, persistent discrimination continues to stand in stark contrast to its cultivated national</p><p>image. While reconciliation initiatives hold symbolic value, they frequently fail to deliver</p><p>substantive change. Government reports outlining reconciliation strategies are often undermined</p><p>by political and legal barriers, resulting in limited tangible benefits for Indigenous communities.</p><p>For example, although compensation has been awarded to residential school survivors, many</p><p>Indigenous communities still lack access to clean drinking water—a fundamental human right</p><p>(Government of Canada, 2021).5 The federal government has repeatedly missed its own</p><p>deadlines to address this crisis, and dozens of communities remain under long-term boil-water</p><p>advisories. Public education initiatives about Indigenous history further exemplify this</p><p>contradiction. While Canadian schools increasingly incorporate Indigenous history into their</p><p>curriculum, many reserves continue to struggle with unreliable access to education (Government</p><p>of Canada, 2018). Similarly, on the topic of land rights, disputes such as the Wet’suwet’en</p><p>opposition to pipeline construction on unceded territory (Amnesty International, 2023) and the</p><p>logging of Fairy Creek Pahcheedaht old-growth forests (Cox, 2021), highlight the government’s</p><p>5 #sourcequality: This is a strong application of #sourcequality because the paragraph uses authoritative and credible sources that enhance the validity of my claims. The use of the Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Canada (2015) provides a foundational and well-vetted document that is central to discussions of reconciliation efforts. Similarly, referencing the Government of Canada (2021) adds current and official data to highlight the gap between stated commitments and actual outcomes. Both sources are current and highly relevant, with accurate and verified information that supports the critique of reconciliation efforts.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 6\"><p>ongoing disregard for Indigenous perspectives. Despite decades of advocacy and claims of</p><p>reconciliation, Indigenous peoples&#x27; lands are still being taken without consent, and their rights</p><p>continue to be trampled under the weight of economic interests.</p><p>In addition to these material inequities, the enduring legacies of intergenerational trauma</p><p>caused by violent colonization remain inadequately addressed. The ongoing crisis of Missing and</p><p>Murdered Indigenous Women and Girls (MMIWG) is a prominent example of systemic neglect</p><p>as reports and calls for action continue to be met with insufficient responses, leaving countless</p><p>families without justice or closure (Native Hope, 2024). Likewise, the substance abuse epidemic,</p><p>driven by cycles of poverty and trauma, is often framed as an internal issue within Indigenous</p><p>communities rather than as a systemic failure requiring national attention and support</p><p>(Urbanoski, 2017). These realities—alongside countless other examples of disparity—highlight</p><p>the profound gap between Canada’s rhetorical commitment to reconciliation and its failure to</p><p>enact meaningful, structural change. While reconciliation efforts have brought visibility to some</p><p>issues, they have not yet dismantled the systemic oppression that continues to marginalize</p><p>Indigenous peoples. Without addressing these foundational inequities and prioritizing the needs</p><p>and rights of Indigenous peoples, Canada’s genuine commitment to justice will remain in</p><p>question, and reconciliation will remain an unfulfilled promise.6</p><p>In closing, Canada&#x27;s image as a harmonious and inclusive society is built on a selective</p><p>narrative that glosses over its colonial roots and the systemic marginalization of Indigenous</p><p>6 #composition: This passage from “These realities…” demonstrates a clear and precise writing style that effectively communicates the argument, in line with #composition. The language is direct and bold, avoiding passive voice, stilted diction, or unnecessary complexity. Transitions between ideas are smooth and the sentences respects the principle of parsimony by using concise wording while maintaining clarity. Furthermore, the choice (above this passage) to paraphrase complex issues rather than overloading the text with quotations creates a seamless integration of ideas.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 7\"><p>peoples. This idealized portrayal of multiculturalism and reconciliation often obscures a harsh</p><p>reality: the nation’s failure to adequately address the ongoing impacts of colonialism. Despite</p><p>gestures toward inclusivity, the continued exploitation of Indigenous lands from unfair treaties,</p><p>the legacy of residential schools, and the neglect of critical issues like Missing and Murdered</p><p>Indigenous Women and Girls (MMIWG) reveal a significant gap between rhetoric and reality.</p><p>The implications of this failure are profound, as it jeopardizes not only the credibility of</p><p>Canada’s national identity but also its capacity to achieve genuine social cohesion and justice. By</p><p>perpetuating an incomplete narrative, Canada risks entrenching historical injustices and</p><p>deepening divisions rather than healing them.</p><p>True reconciliation requires more than just symbolic gestures or partial acknowledgment</p><p>of past harms. It demands systemic change, including the implementation of policies that uphold</p><p>Indigenous sovereignty, reparations for historical injustices, and the dismantling of institutional</p><p>structures that perpetuate inequality. Moreover, reconciliation must involve a genuine partnership</p><p>with Indigenous communities, prioritizing their voices in shaping the future of the nation. Until</p><p>Canada fully acknowledges the scope of its colonial legacy and takes meaningful steps to rectify</p><p>these disparities, its claims of justice and reconciliation will remain unfulfilled promises. By</p><p>confronting these uncomfortable truths and addressing the deep-seated inequalities within its</p><p>borders, Canada can move beyond its idealized image and transform its aspirational identity into</p><p>a lived reality all while upholding the rights and dignity of Indigenous peoples.7</p><p>WORD COUNT: 1422 WORDS</p><p>7 #professionalism: Overall my completion of this assignment shows #professionalism because it demonstrates a thorough and thoughtful approach by using all the HCs effectively. The work adheres to all formatting guidelines, with attention to detail and respect for academic standards. Starting the assignment early allowed for careful planning and execution, avoiding last-minute rushing and ensuring high-quality output. The word count was maintained and additionally, the work draws on course learnings, prior pre-works, and feedback from classes, showing a deep engagement with the material and a commitment to continuous improvement.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 8\"><p>AI STATEMENT</p><p>In this assignment, I used minimal AI assistance because of my deep passion and</p><p>extensive pre-existing knowledge about the topic. This subject is important to me, and I wanted</p><p>to ensure my essay strongly reflected my personal stance and understanding. Exploring Canada’s</p><p>national identity, its colonial legacy, and the ongoing marginalization of Indigenous peoples</p><p>required a nuanced and informed perspective, which I aimed to provide through my own</p><p>experiences and research.</p><p>Furthermore, the niche nature of this topic posed challenges for relying on AI tools, as</p><p>they often provide broad or generalized information that lacks the specificity and context</p><p>required for my argument. Many examples in my essay, such as the Fairy Creek protest,</p><p>substance abuse, access to clean water, and education challenges on reserves, were drawn from</p><p>personal discussions and lived experiences. For instance, my involvement with the Pacheedaht</p><p>alliances to protect old-growth forests at Fairy Creek deeply informed my analysis. Additionally,</p><p>my understanding of governmental documents like the Charter of Rights and Freedoms and</p><p>reconciliation efforts stems from my upbringing and education in Canada, where these topics</p><p>were frequently explored in school.</p><p>While AI played a minor role, I did use it for some more tedious tasks. For example, I</p><p>used ChatGPT to summarize lengthy research papers (after reading them) to help me create ideas</p><p>for annotations. I used MyBib for citation formatting and Grammarly/Google Docs spell-checker</p><p>for grammar and spell-checking. These tools helped me refine my writing, but the core</p><p>arguments, examples, and structure came entirely from my own knowledge, hopefully reflecting</p><p>my authentic voice and perspective.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 9\"><p>ANNOTATED BIBLIOGRAPHY</p><p>Amnesty International. (2023, December 11). Criminalization, unlawful surveillance of</p><p>Wet’suwet’en land defenders. Amnesty International.</p><p>https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2023/12/canada-amnesty-criminalization-surveill</p><p>ance-wetsuweten-land-defenders/</p><p>This report by Amnesty International highlights the criminalization and surveillance</p><p>faced by Wet’suwet’en land defenders who oppose the construction of the Coastal GasLink</p><p>pipeline on unceded territory. Amnesty International critiques the Canadian government&#x27;s</p><p>actions, suggesting that they violate Indigenous rights and freedoms. This document sheds light</p><p>on the ongoing resistance to government-sanctioned development projects on Indigenous lands,</p><p>framing them as both a legal and human rights issue.</p><p>Austen, I., &amp; Bracken, A. (2024, September 20). Unmarked Graves at Canada’s Former</p><p>Indigenous Schools Fuel A Debate. Nytimes.com; The New York Times.</p><p>https://www.nytimes.com/2024/09/20/canada-indigenous-schools-unmarked-graves.html</p><p>This article from The New York Times discusses the discovery of unmarked graves at</p><p>former residential school sites across Canada, a finding that has intensified debates over</p><p>Canada’s colonial legacy. Austen and Bracken provide an overview of the historical context of</p><p>residential schools, the trauma inflicted upon Indigenous communities, and the political</p><p>responses to these discoveries, raising questions about national reconciliation efforts. The article</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 10\"><p>also explores the broader societal implications of these revelations, including their impact on</p><p>public consciousness and Canada’s self-identity.</p><p>Cox, S. (2021, July 1). Inside the Pacheedaht Nation’s stand on Fairy Creek logging blockades.</p><p>The Narwhal. https://thenarwhal.ca/pacheedaht-fairy-creek-bc-logging/</p><p>Cox&#x27;s article in The Narwhal provides an in-depth look at the Pacheedaht Nation’s</p><p>involvement in and views on the Fairy Creek logging blockades, where environmentalists and</p><p>Indigenous land defenders are trying to protect one of Canada’s last remaining old-growth</p><p>forests. It examines the complex dynamics between Indigenous rights, environmental protection,</p><p>and economic interests, analyzing the tension between conservation efforts and resource</p><p>extraction. Additionally, it highlights internal divisions within the Pacheedaht Nation, illustrating</p><p>how Indigenous communities face unique pressures when navigating external activism and</p><p>economic opportunities.</p><p>Government of Canada. (2019). The Canadian Charter of Rights and Freedoms. Justice.gc.ca;</p><p>Government of Canada. https://www.justice.gc.ca/eng/csj-sjc/rfc-dlc/ccrf-ccdl/</p><p>This official government document outlines the Canadian Charter of Rights and</p><p>Freedoms, which is a fundamental legal foundation for individual rights in Canada. While not</p><p>specific to Indigenous issues, the Charter’s principles are relevant to Indigenous rights, as</p><p>Indigenous communities use it to argue for protections and freedoms that are sometimes</p><p>overlooked in favor of development projects or political agendas. The Charter is also invoked in</p><p>legal battles addressing systemic inequities, illustrating its potential to challenge colonial</p><p>legacies and advance Indigenous sovereignty within Canada&#x27;s legal framework.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 11\"><p>Government of Canada, O. of the A. G. of C. (2018, May 29). Report 5—Socio-economic Gaps</p><p>on First Nations Reserves—Indigenous Services Canada. Www.oag-Bvg.gc.ca.</p><p>https://www.oag-bvg.gc.ca/internet/English/parl_oag_201805_05_e_43037.html</p><p>This report from the Office of the Auditor General of Canada evaluates the</p><p>socio-economic disparities facing First Nations communities, particularly on reserves. It reveals</p><p>significant gaps in health, education, and economic opportunities for Indigenous peoples,</p><p>attributing many of these issues to inadequate government support and policies. This report is</p><p>frequently referenced in discussions of systemic inequality affecting Indigenous populations.</p><p>Government of Canada, O. of the A. G. of C. (2021, February 25). Report 3—Access to Safe</p><p>Drinking Water in First Nations Communities—Indigenous Services Canada.</p><p>Www.oag-Bvg.gc.ca.</p><p>https://www.oag-bvg.gc.ca/internet/English/parl_oag_202102_03_e_43749.html</p><p>This governmental report addresses the critical issue of water access on First Nations</p><p>reserves, acknowledging the government’s repeated failure to ensure safe drinking water for all</p><p>Indigenous communities. The report examines the reasons behind these persistent boil-water</p><p>advisories and proposes recommendations, highlighting the gap between governmental promises</p><p>and the realities on reserves. It highlights how systemic barriers, such as inadequate</p><p>infrastructure and underfunded services, perpetuate health and safety risks in these communities,</p><p>calling for urgent and sustained action to close these gaps.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 12\"><p>Hall, A. J. (2011, June 6). Treaties with Indigenous Peoples in Canada | The Canadian</p><p>Encyclopedia. The Canadian Encyclopedia.</p><p>https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/aboriginal-treaties</p><p>Hall’s article provides an overview of treaties between Indigenous peoples and the</p><p>Canadian government, covering historical contexts and contemporary challenges. Hall</p><p>emphasizes the power imbalances and misunderstandings during treaty negotiations, which often</p><p>resulted in Indigenous peoples being deprived of their lands. This source is foundational for</p><p>understanding the historical basis of land disputes in Canada.</p><p>Miller, J. R. (2012, October 10). Residential Schools in Canada. The Canadian Encyclopedia;</p><p>Historica Canada. https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/residential-schools</p><p>This source provides an overview of the history and legacy of residential schools in</p><p>Canada, detailing their establishment, purpose, and impact on Indigenous communities. Miller</p><p>explores how these institutions were a central tool of colonial assimilation policies, designed to</p><p>eradicate Indigenous cultures, languages, and traditions. The article also discusses the long-term</p><p>consequences of residential schools, including intergenerational trauma and ongoing challenges</p><p>faced by Indigenous peoples, valuable for understanding the systemic nature of colonialism in</p><p>Canada.</p><p>Native Hope. (2024). Missing and Murdered Indigenous Women (MMIW). Www.nativehope.org;</p><p>Native Hope.</p><p>https://www.nativehope.org/missing-and-murdered-indigenous-women-mmiw</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 13\"><p>Native Hope’s publication on Missing and Murdered Indigenous Women (MMIW)</p><p>discusses the systemic issues contributing to the high rates of violence against Indigenous</p><p>women and girls in Canada. It highlights social, economic, and political factors that perpetuate</p><p>this violence and the lack of adequate government response, emphasizing the need for national</p><p>accountability and systemic reform. The publication also explores the cultural and</p><p>intergenerational impacts of this crisis, calling for broader societal awareness and the integration</p><p>of Indigenous-led solutions to address the root causes effectively.</p><p>Parrott, Z. (2006, February 7). Indian Act. The Canadian Encyclopedia; The Canadian</p><p>Encyclopedia. https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/indian-act</p><p>Parrott&#x27;s article on the Indian Act provides insight into the Canadian legal framework that</p><p>has historically governed Indigenous peoples’ lives. The Indian Act is critiqued for its oppressive</p><p>policies that sought to assimilate Indigenous communities. Parrott explains how this act</p><p>contributed to longstanding issues within Indigenous communities, including loss of autonomy</p><p>and cultural suppression.</p><p>Srikanth, H. (2012). Multiculturalism and the Aboriginal Peoples in Canada. Economic and</p><p>Political Weekly, 47(23), 17–21. http://www.jstor.org/stable/23214913</p><p>In this journal article, Srikanth examines Canada’s approach to multiculturalism and its</p><p>implications for Indigenous communities. Srikanth argues that while multicultural policies aim to</p><p>include diverse cultures, they often fail to address the unique political and cultural rights of</p><p>Indigenous peoples. The article critiques Canadian multiculturalism as being insufficient in</p><p>reconciling Indigenous sovereignty and cultural preservation.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 14\"><p>Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Canada. (2015). Honouring the Truth, Reconciling for</p><p>the Future Honouring the Truth, Reconciling for the Future Summary of the Final Report</p><p>of the Truth and Reconciliation Commission of Canada The Truth and Reconciliation</p><p>Commission of Canada.</p><p>https://irsi.ubc.ca/sites/default/files/inline-files/Executive_Summary_English_Web.pdf</p><p>This foundational report summarizes the findings of the Truth and Reconciliation</p><p>Commission, which investigated the history and legacy of residential schools in Canada. It</p><p>outlines calls to action aimed at redressing past injustices and promoting reconciliation. The</p><p>report has been instrumental in shaping public policy and dialogue around Indigenous rights and</p><p>Canadian reconciliation efforts.</p><p>Urbanoski, K. A. (2017). Need for equity in treatment of substance use among Indigenous people</p><p>in Canada. Canadian Medical Association Journal, 189(44), E1350–E1351.</p><p>https://doi.org/10.1503/cmaj.171002</p><p>In this publication for the Canadian Medical Association Journal, Urbanoski discusses</p><p>the inequities in substance use treatment access for Indigenous people in Canada, emphasizing</p><p>the need for culturally appropriate healthcare. The article highlights systemic barriers, such as</p><p>lack of funding and cultural misunderstanding, that prevent Indigenous communities from</p><p>receiving equitable substance use support, emphasizing a need for healthcare reforms. Urbanoski</p><p>also calls for the incorporation of Indigenous knowledge and practices into treatment programs,</p><p>advocating for approaches that respect and address the cultural and historical context of</p><p>Indigenous health disparities.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 15\"><p>Wayland, S. V. (1997). Immigration, Multiculturalism and National Identity in Canada.</p><p>International Journal on Minority and Group Rights, 5(1), 33–58.</p><p>http://www.jstor.org/stable/24674516</p><p>Wayland’s article examines Canada’s national identity through the lens of</p><p>multiculturalism and immigration policy, addressing how these policies intersect with Indigenous</p><p>rights. Wayland argues that while Canada celebrates diversity, Indigenous concerns are often</p><p>sidelined, as multicultural policies tend to favor immigrant communities over the needs and</p><p>rights of Indigenous peoples. The article further critiques how this imbalance reflects a broader</p><p>pattern of marginalization, wherein Indigenous sovereignty and historical injustices are</p><p>overshadowed by narratives that prioritize Canada’s image as a welcoming and inclusive nation.</p></section>"}, {"id": "passdown", "title": "ŦÁ¸WEN Passdown Ceremony", "date": "", "tag": "WRITING", "excerpt": "Full text from ŦÁ¸WEN PASSDOWN CEREMONY.pdf", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"writing/passdown.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read the original PDF ↗</a> · Full text, including references and assignment notes. Figures are available in the PDF.</p><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 1\"><p>TAWEN PASSDOWN CEREMONY</p><p>Welcome to the ŦÁ¸WEN Passdown Ceremony. This event is steeped in tradition and meaning, and though it may be long, I promise it will be a memorable and heartfelt experience.</p><p>Before we dive into the ceremony, I want to start with an activity that echoes the spirit of gratitude we share during our house meetings, this is also reminiscent of an activity we did during the YR49 first orientation week with Rebecca. This lovely activity was suggested by Sam.</p><p>I&#x27;ll begin with this ball of yarn, connecting it to my wrist. I&#x27;ll share my favourite thing about this house, something I&#x27;m grateful for, or something I&#x27;m going to miss about this ŦÁ¸WEN cohort. Then, I&#x27;ll toss the yarn to someone else, who will do the same. By the end, we&#x27;ll all be connected, a symbolic reminder of our bond and shared memories.</p><p>[ YARN ACTIVITY ]</p><p>Now that we&#x27;ve created our web of gratitude, we can proceed with the ceremony. This tradition has been passed down through the ages, from the earliest generations to us today. Each passdown is filled with meaning and love, as cherished items are given by second years to the next generation of the ŦÁ¸WEN family. While it might be hard to let go of these treasured objects, it is now your turn to receive them, to hold them dear, and to carry forward the legacy of our house. We will begin the ceremony from here. Each second year will stand up, one by one, with their passdowns ready to give away. First years, please stand up to receive your passdowns and share a hug with your second years. We will continue this process until everyone has had their turn, completing the circle. House royalty will be given last.</p><p>Let&#x27;s embrace this moment together, honouring the past and looking forward to the future. Welcome to the ŦÁ¸WEN Passdown Ceremony.</p><p>[ PASSDOWN CEREMONY ]</p><p>Speech about being house spirit, what it means to me, what it means for the future and who i got it from</p><p>blah blah blah</p><p>[ GO TO THE TREE ]</p><p>We come back to this tree, and instead of donating sticks, I want you all to find a branch or leaf to stand by and fan this tree with words of gratitude and hope. As you do this, look around at your new second-year cohort and give all your love and support to the growth of this tree, and therefore our house. Once you&#x27;re done, you may sit down, and we will continue the ceremony.</p><p>[ SITTING DOWN, POST-TREE ACTIVITY ]</p><p>We gather at this tree, a symbol of the continuous growth of not only Tawen House but of all of us as individuals and as a family. What we put into this tree mirrors what we put into our community,</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 2\"><p>contributing to its proliferation and continuity. Jordan taught me a word: ephemeral. I think it&#x27;s the perfect word to sum up the Pearson journey. Year 49, we first met here last year at our initiation with our second years. We then came back to close off the year with our second years doing this very tradition. Then, we became the second years and gathered with our first years here for their initiation. And now, we end our journey as an ephemeral cohort. The specialness of this family lies in the time we gather together. This tree, standing tall and steadfast, has witnessed our moments of joy, laughter, tears, and growth. It has seen us at our most vulnerable and our most triumphant. Every gathering here, every tradition, every story shared under its branches has woven us together into a tapestry of memories that will forever be a part of us.</p><p>As we stand here today, let us remember the roots we&#x27;ve planted—not just in this soil but in each other&#x27;s hearts. The roots of friendships that have grown deeper with every shared experience, the roots of knowledge that have expanded with every lesson learned, and the roots of love that have flourished with every act of kindness. These houses mean something different to everyone. While they represent the broad elements of earth, air, fire, the moon, and of course water, their significance to each individual varies. To get off script a bit: to me, this house is my home and family. I left home scared to be alone, but I realized that in this house, I&#x27;m never really alone. From dayroom talks to screams in the forest to gossip sessions on the floor, every experience has provided me with more depth than any other part of my life. These two years have truly been the best years of my life. I&#x27;ve gained a family that will stay with me no matter where we all go. The amount of love I have for this house and everyone in it is unquantifiable. No matter where I go or where any of us go, we will always be tied back to this very house.</p><p>Ephemeral, ephemeral reminds us of the fleeting nature of time. Our days at Pearson, though short, are filled with moments that shape us, define us, and prepare us for the world beyond these walls. We have learned to cherish each moment, to embrace the present, and to understand that while our time here may be brief, its impact is everlasting.</p><p>We are a family forged in the fire of shared experiences, bound by the laughter that echoed through these halls, and strengthened by the challenges we faced together. The bonds we&#x27;ve formed here are truly unbreakable, and the memories we&#x27;ve created are treasures we will carry with us always. Even As we prepare to embark on new journeys, let us carry the spirit of Tawen House with us. To my fellow Year 49s, thank you for being a part of this incredible journey. Thank you for the late-night conversations, the spontaneous adventures, the unwavering support, and the endless love. Each of you has left an indelible mark on my heart, and for that, I am eternally grateful. And to my Year 50s, thank you for the reinvigorated spirit you brought. Your energy, passion, and friendships have all supplied me with so much hope and joy. Each of you has added a unique spark to our house, making it more vibrant and alive. Thank you in advance for continuing not only this legacy but also the legacy of generations. Your contributions will shape the future of our house, and I have no doubt that you will carry forward the values and love that have defined us.</p><p>Let us leave this place not with sadness, but with hope and excitement for the future. Let us remember that, like this tree, we will continue to grow and flourish, nourished by the love and lessons we&#x27;ve shared here. Our journey may be ephemeral, but its impact is timeless.</p></section>"}, {"id": "cas-week", "title": "CAS Week Reflection", "date": "", "tag": "WRITING", "excerpt": "Full text from CAS week reflection.docx.pdf", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"writing/cas-week.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read the original PDF ↗</a> · Full text, including references and assignment notes. Figures are available in the PDF.</p><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 1\"><p>CAS week reflection</p><p>For my CAS week, I participated in the intramural/classical photography CAS week. For our CAS week, we went to Tofino to participate in many place-based activities. On the first day, we were able to go to the Malahat skywalk on the road to Tofino. There, we went on a walk through a scenic view full of tall forests, crystal blue waters and a sunny/clear sky. Going up the skywalk, some of us conquered our fears of heights and walked up a 32-metre spiral staircase to look over the Saanich peninsula and connected mountains. It was definitely a highlight of the trip. From the wind blowing in our faces with the refreshing feeling of the ocean air, it just felt like we were really immersed in the natural surroundings. I could see smiles on everyone&#x27;s faces not only through my own eyes but also through the pictures we took with our film cameras!</p><p>The following day was the main event of our trip, surfing. At first, I was really nervous but also excited. I had surfed once before but never in cold water with wet suits. In addition, the weather was not in our favour so the waves were predicted to be pretty ravenous. However, despite the worries, I decided to focus my thoughts more on the new adventure and bonding experience I&#x27;d be having. In the morning, we got our wetsuits on and met up with the Surf sisters (our instructors) and we walked out to the beach with our surfboards. We got a quick lesson on the motions we needed to go through in order to stand on the board and then we got in the water. Thankfully, the wetsuits were very insulating because the water was freezing, plus the rain and wind were pretty extreme. The waves were pretty &quot;gnarly&quot; and rapid so my instructor Alyssa helped me catch my first wave. I set myself up on the board and waited for her cue when I started paddling. (The female representation and leaders also felt very empowering!) When I felt the strong push from the water, I stood up. That one moment made the cold weather worth it. Even though the water and wind were really working against us, being able to stand for a few seconds felt like we were floating on top of the water.</p><p>After the first wave, I was on my own. From this, I really learned about determination and patience. Because of the strong, tall and continuous waves, it was really hard to stand up without completely nose-diving into the water. However, in my opinion, the fun part included the many trial and errors of falling off your board, getting up and setting yourself up to do it again. After flopping into the water, rather than frustration I would laugh at myself because I realized that treating myself badly would only make it more difficult and less fun to keep trying. So, instead, I just maintained a determined attitude and focused on the transition from kneeling to standing. After multiple (very funny) failed attempts, I stood up by myself. It made me feel so accomplished because I achieved my goal with my own effort. After that, I was able to stand up four more times. After the lesson, I felt so successful and proud of myself and it was really a great learning experience about gaining new skills through hard work and determination.</p><p>The next highlight of the Tofino adventure was the following day when we took a water taxi to Meares island. Meares island is some of the last completely on-logged old-growth forests in the world. Upon arriving on the island, we began walking along a boardwalk tour led by Travis. I learned about old-growth trees, redwood cedar and the importance they have to not only the Indigenous people of the island but also the status of health of the earth in general. Travis told us about how old growth is essential to keeping forests healthy because the monoculture tree farms only result in unnatural side effects such as the increase of forest fires. I also learned about the role of the Tla-o-qui-aht people in saving and preserving Meares island. They were the biggest contributor to the fairy creek protest (a historic and large movement in Canada to protect old-growth forests). If it wasn’t for the work and activism of these individuals, all of the old-growth forests would have been logged because each tree is around 50,000 dollars in producer value. This tour from Travis connected me to nature, and I felt grounded. In his teachings, I learned about Indigenous culture and their connection to the environment and how we can use their ways of life to protect the nature that surrounds us.</p><p>While on the tour, Travis even brought us on a trek off the boardwalk (it was extreme hiking, it felt like wilderness CAS). We really pushed ourselves, physically and mentally. We had to climb through small passageways, jump over fallen trees, climb up steep rock cliffs and slide down muddy grounds. In the end, we were all soaked and covered in mud. Even though it was really difficult, it was so much fun. It was definitely a bonding experience. We persevered through physical obstacles such as climbing up giant rocks and also through problems such as losing half our group. In the end, everyone felt very accomplished (and tired), it was a great immersive experience!</p><p>Overall the CAS experience was amazing. It is definitely one of the most memorable and fun moments from Pearson so far. The organized chaos pushed me out of my comfort zone. I learned physical skills such as surfing, hiking, rock climbing and even golfing. I also learned mental skills such as how to stay determined, the importance of courage and perseverance and the value of patience. I was able to bond with my peers and create once-in-a-lifetime memories whilst also recording them through film!</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 2\"></section>"}, {"id": "project-week", "title": "Project Week Reflection", "date": "", "tag": "WRITING", "excerpt": "Full text from Project Week Reflection.docx.pdf", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"writing/project-week.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read the original PDF ↗</a> · Full text, including references and assignment notes. Figures are available in the PDF.</p><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 1\"><p>Project Week Reflection</p><p>As I sit here reflecting on my unforgettable project week adventure, I can&#x27;t help but be filled with a sense of awe and gratitude for the incredible journey I experienced. The snowshoeing expedition to Kludahk Ridge was one of the most challenging and rewarding weeks of my life. It pushed me beyond my limits, both physically and mentally, but the sense of accomplishment and the bonds forged along the way made every step worth it. From the very beginning, as we gathered in the early morning hours and made our way to the trailhead, the anticipation was tangible. The crisp winter air and the stunning snow-covered scenery set the stage for the adventure that awaited us. With snowshoes strapped on and gear adjusted, our group embarked on the ascent up the ridge, brimming with excitement and determination.</p><p>The journey itself was nothing short of incredible. We walked for eight hours a day, covering a total distance of 50 kilometres and ascending 1000 meters through two meters of snow. The physical exertion was intense, and each step forward demanded strength and perseverance. But with each challenging obstacle, whether it was a steep incline or a tricky descent, we grew closer as a group and found within ourselves the resilience to push beyond our perceived limits.</p><p>The breathtaking views of snow-capped mountains and forests were a constant reminder of the beauty that surrounded us. Despite the occasional blizzard and the harsh conditions, the sun cast its golden rays upon us, creating long shadows that followed our footsteps. The combination of physical exertion and stunning natural scenery created a sense of awe and appreciation for the wonders of the world around us. But beyond the physical challenges and the awe-inspiring landscapes, it was the people I shared this journey with that made it truly remarkable. Bonding with my fellow adventurers, we supported and encouraged one another through every hurdle. Together, we navigated treacherous terrain, celebrated small victories, and provided solace during moments of fatigue and doubt. The shared experience forged deep connections, creating a sense of camaraderie and friendship that will last a lifetime.</p><p>As we reached our campsite each night, exhausted but exhilarated, we took the time to immerse ourselves in the tranquillity and natural beauty that surrounded us. It was a chance to disconnect from the noise and distractions of everyday life and embrace the simplicity and wonder of the wilderness. It was in those quiet moments that we found solace and a profound appreciation for the world we inhabit. Looking back, this project week&#x27;s adventure was undoubtedly one of the most amazing weeks of my life. The physical challenges tested my limits, but they also revealed my inner strength and resilience. The stunning landscapes and the sense of awe they inspired reminded me of the beauty that exists beyond our daily routines. And most importantly, the bonds formed with my fellow adventurers created a sense of community and connection that will forever hold a special place in my heart.</p><p>As I continue to share the rest of this incredible journey through my upcoming videos, I hope to convey the immense joy, growth, and inspiration that snowshoeing on Kludahk Ridge brought into my life. It was a week filled with challenges, breathtaking scenery, and the forging of lifelong friendships. I am grateful for the opportunity to embark on such an adventure, and I can confidently say that this project week will always hold a cherished place in my memories.</p></section>"}, {"id": "wang-ximeng", "title": "Analysis of “A Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains”: Wang Ximeng’s Ode to Daoist Harmony and Dynasty Idealism", "date": "", "tag": "WRITING", "excerpt": "Full text from Stephanie HL ESSAY FINAL DRAFT.docx.pdf", "body": "<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"writing/wang-ximeng.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read the original PDF ↗</a> · Full text, including references and assignment notes. Figures are available in the PDF.</p><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 1\"><p>Higher Level Essay</p><p>English A: Language and Literature</p><p>Title: Analysis of &quot;A Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains&quot;: Wang Ximeng&#x27;s</p><p>Ode to Daoist Harmony and Dynasty Idealism</p><p>International Baccalaureate Organization</p><p>Word Count: 1342</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 2\"><p>Wang Ximeng&#x27;s &quot;A Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains&quot; stands as a pinnacle in Chinese</p><p>visual art, renowned for its intricate details and captivating landscapes, carrying profound cultural</p><p>significance. Examining elements such as the color palette, shifting perspectives, and textual medium</p><p>allows for a close analysis of Wang Ximeng&#x27;s meticulous symbolism linked to both dynasty longevity</p><p>and Daoist pursuits. (&quot;Wang Ximeng&#x27;s &#x27;One Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains&#x27;&quot; - China Online</p><p>Museum) Wang Ximeng was an artist during the Northern Song Dynasty, this era was marked by</p><p>relative peace and prosperity, as well as significant cultural and artistic advancements (&quot;Song</p><p>Dynasty&quot; - Britannica). Daoism played a significant role in shaping the cultural and philosophical</p><p>landscape of the Northern Song Dynasty. Daoism is a major Chinese philosophical and spiritual</p><p>tradition that emerged alongside Confucianism and Buddhism. It places a strong emphasis on living in</p><p>harmony with the natural order, promoting values such as simplicity, spontaneity, and balance. A core</p><p>principle of Daoism is the concept of harmony with nature. Artists like Wang Ximeng sought to</p><p>capture the beauty of nature and convey a sense of tranquillity and balance in their works. Through</p><p>Wang Ximeng&#x27;s “A Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains”, this essay will show how authorial</p><p>choices such as colour palette and text medium within the work encapsulates Daoist principles,</p><p>notably the idea of harmony with nature. It will also investigate how the use of landscape conveys the</p><p>thematic exploration of collapsed dreams and the idealism associated with the longevity of dynasties.</p><p>Wang Ximeng&#x27;s &quot;A Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains&quot; is a masterclass in composition</p><p>and symbolism, transcending mere artistic expression to become a visual manifestation of Daoist</p><p>principles. The landscape is intricately designed, showcasing a sprawling panorama of landscapes that</p><p>extend almost 12 meters, inviting viewers to delve into its meticulous details (Fig 1. Full Artwork)</p><p>The mountains, depicted with a defocused blending technique, emerge from the gradient, symbolizing</p><p>the steadfast and timeless nature of Daoist ideals such as wu wei (Fig 2. Mountain Focus). Embodying</p><p>the Daoist concept of wu wei, they stand as stoic guardians, suggesting that true strength lies in being</p><p>in harmony with one&#x27;s natural state (&quot;Wu Wei&quot; - Britannica). The rivers winding through the</p><p>landscape symbolize the continuous flow of life, echoing the Daoist belief in embracing the natural</p><p>course of events. The choice of structures and figures seamlessly integrated into the scenery reinforces</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 3\"><p>the idea of interconnectedness. Figures engaged in daily activities are not mere adornments but</p><p>essential components of the harmonious whole, illustrating the Daoist principle of balance in human</p><p>endeavours.</p><p>Furthermore the use of scrolls in &quot;A Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains&quot; goes beyond</p><p>being a practical choice; it is a deliberate strategy enriching the painting with meaning and aligning it</p><p>with Daoist philosophy. In Northern Song culture, scrolls were not just conveyors of flexible and</p><p>portable Daoist thought but integral to daily life, scholarship, and traditions. The elongated scroll</p><p>format, characteristic of Daoist paintings, mirrors the fluidity and adaptability of Daoist philosophy.</p><p>Unrolling the scroll becomes a metaphorical journey, revealing the landscape gradually, echoing the</p><p>Daoist emphasis on the ever-changing nature of existence (&quot;Daoism and Daoist Philosophy&quot; -</p><p>Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy). Beyond its artistic role, the scroll embodies Daoist principles</p><p>of embracing change and impermanence. The act of physically unrolling the scroll involves the</p><p>viewer in a process of unfolding, celebrating the fluidity inherent in Daoist philosophy. In the cultural</p><p>context, scrolls were not just artistic expressions but repositories of knowledge, accessible and</p><p>cherished, weaving Daoist ideals into the fabric of society.</p><p>The colour palette used in the painting contributes to its emotional resonance. The vibrant</p><p>azurite blue dominating the mountain peaks, complemented by the bold malachite green in the</p><p>valleys, creates a visually striking contrast (Fig 3. Colour Focus). This use of colour not only captures</p><p>the viewer&#x27;s attention but also mirrors the Daoist emphasis on the dynamic interplay of opposites,</p><p>where vibrant hues and subtle shades coexist in a harmonious blend. The pale ochre brown at the</p><p>mountain bases adds a grounding element, reinforcing the interconnected relationship between the</p><p>celestial and earthly realms. The shifting perspective and the &quot;three-distance&quot; technique are critical</p><p>aspects of the composition (Fig 4. Three-Distance Technique). The viewer, unrolling the scroll from</p><p>right to left, experiences a dynamic narrative akin to a journey through time and space. This technique</p><p>aligns with Daoist themes of change and transformation, emphasizing the impermanence of the</p><p>natural world. The &quot;Three Distances&quot; formula (&quot;SHAN-SHUI-HUA&quot; – Core.ac.uk), with its visual</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 4\"><p>angles of gaoyuan (high-distance), shenyuan (deep-distance), and pingyuan (level-distance), creates a</p><p>sense of panoramic scale, reinforcing the Daoist idea that nature is vast and all-encompassing.</p><p>The painting serves as a visual testament to the profound influence of Daoism during the</p><p>Northern Song Dynasty. Daoist principles are not merely illustrated but are woven into the fabric of</p><p>the artwork itself. The interconnectedness of elements within the composition reflects the Daoist</p><p>belief in the unity of the natural world, human society, and philosophical principles. Mountains and</p><p>rivers become living symbols of Dao, the underlying force that governs the cosmos. The mountains,</p><p>with their undulating peaks and valleys, echo the Daoist concept of the ever-changing nature of</p><p>existence. They stand as silent witnesses to the cyclical patterns of nature, embodying the Daoist ideal</p><p>of living in harmony with the natural order. The rivers, flowing serenely through the landscape,</p><p>represent the eternal flux of life, reinforcing the Daoist emphasis on embracing change with grace and</p><p>humility. The presence of structures and figures in the painting further emphasizes the integration of</p><p>Daoist thought into daily life. These elements are not imposed on the landscape but seamlessly blend</p><p>with it, suggesting a harmonious coexistence between humanity and nature. Daoism, with its emphasis</p><p>on simplicity, spontaneity, and balance, becomes a guiding principle that shapes both the artistic and</p><p>societal aspects of the Northern Song Dynasty.</p><p>As the viewer delves deeper into &quot;A Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains,&quot; the layers of the</p><p>painting unveil a poignant commentary on the theme of collapsed dreams and the idealism of dynasty</p><p>longevity. Emperor Huizong&#x27;s Daoist beliefs and his interpretation of the artwork as an almost Daoist</p><p>fairyland (&quot;One Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains&quot; DailyArt Magazine) add a layer of complexity</p><p>to the narrative, inviting contemplation on the delicate interplay between artistic expression and</p><p>historical realities. The serene landscape depicted in the painting becomes a canvas of hope,</p><p>portraying the emperor&#x27;s aspirations for a tranquil and prosperous countryside and contributing to the</p><p>creation of an idyllic realm. However, beneath this optimistic interpretation lies a metaphor for</p><p>Emperor Huizong&#x27;s longevity and the dynasty&#x27;s everlasting prosperity (“Emperor Huizong of Song” -</p><p>New World Encyclopedia). Yet, the idealism inherent in this artistic representation serves as the focal</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 5\"><p>point of the theme of collapsed dreams. The Daoist fairyland, visually enchanting, embodies a utopian</p><p>vision detached from the harsh realities of history. The historical irony in &quot;A Thousand Li of Rivers</p><p>and Mountains&quot; deepens with Emperor Huizong&#x27;s forced abdication in 1126, marking the Northern</p><p>Song Dynasty&#x27;s collapse in 1127. Owner Prime Minister Cai Jing&#x27;s disgrace as a corrupt official</p><p>contrasts with the unfulfilled dream depicted, highlighting artistic idealism&#x27;s fragility amid political</p><p>turmoil. The juxtaposition of Daoist ideals with historical realities underscores art&#x27;s intricate relation</p><p>to human affairs&#x27; ever-shifting tides. The once hopeful landscape now testifies to empires&#x27;</p><p>impermanence and human aspirations&#x27; transient nature—a reflection of both the emperor&#x27;s dreams and</p><p>society rooted in Daoist philosophy. (&quot;Northern Song Dynasty&quot; - The Metropolitan Museum of Art).</p><p>In conclusion, Wang Ximeng&#x27;s &quot;A Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains&quot; stands as a pinnacle</p><p>in Chinese visual art, celebrated for its intricate details and profound cultural significance. The visual</p><p>analysis of the painting offered a unique insight into Chinese artistic and philosophical heritage,</p><p>unveiling the cultural and historical contexts that influenced Wang Ximeng&#x27;s work. His authorial</p><p>choices encapsulate Daoist principles, notably the idea of harmony with nature, and delve into the</p><p>thematic exploration of collapsed dreams and the idealism associated with dynasty longevity. Wang</p><p>Ximeng&#x27;s portrayal of an idyllic landscape, intertwined with Daoist philosophy, gains poignant</p><p>significance within the historical context. As viewers immerse themselves in the painting, layers of</p><p>meaning unfold, revealing a commentary on the delicate balance between artistic idealism and the</p><p>harsh truths of history. This juxtaposition underscores the intricate relationship between art and the</p><p>ever-shifting tides of human affairs, showcasing the timeless resonance of Daoist principles within the</p><p>context of Wang Ximeng&#x27;s masterful creation.</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 6\"><p>Appendix</p><p>Fig 1. Full Artwork (Split into 4 sections)</p><p>Fig 2. Mountain Focus</p><p>Fig. 3 Color Focus Fig. 4 Three-Distance Technique</p></section><section class=\"imported-page\" aria-label=\"Source page 7\"><p>Citations</p><p>1. “Wang Ximeng: One Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains.” China Online Museum, 8 Dec.</p><p>2015,</p><p>www.comuseum.com/painting/famous-chinese-paintings/wang-ximeng-one-thousand-li-of-riv</p><p>ers-and-mountains/#.</p><p>2. “Song Dynasty.” Encyclopædia Britannica, Encyclopædia Britannica, inc., 19 Jan. 2024,</p><p>www.britannica.com/topic/Song-dynasty.</p><p>3. “Wuwei.” Encyclopædia Britannica, Encyclopædia Britannica, inc.,</p><p>www.britannica.com/topic/wuwei-Chinese-philosophy. Accessed 3 Feb. 2024.</p><p>4. Bolewski, Christin. “‘shan-Shui-Hua’ – Traditional Chinese Landscape Painting ...”</p><p>‘SHAN-SHUI-HUA’ – TRADITIONAL CHINESE LANDSCAPE PAINTING</p><p>REINTERPRETED AS MOVING DIGITAL VISUALISATION,</p><p>core.ac.uk/download/pdf/288357476.pdf. Accessed 4 Feb. 2024.</p><p>5. Singer, James W. “Masterpiece Story: One Thousand Li of Rivers and Mountains by Wang</p><p>Ximeng.” DailyArt Magazine, 3 Nov. 2023,</p><p>www.dailyartmagazine.com/one-thousand-li-of-rivers-and-mountains/.</p><p>6. “Emperor Huizong of Song.” Visit the Main Page,</p><p>www.newworldencyclopedia.org/entry/Emperor_Huizong_of_Song. Accessed 3 Feb. 2024.</p><p>7. Art, Department of Asian. “Northern Song Dynasty (960–1127): Essay: The Metropolitan</p><p>Museum of Art: Heilbrunn Timeline of Art History.” The Met’s Heilbrunn Timeline of Art</p><p>History, 1 Jan. 2001, www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/nsong/hd_nsong.htm.</p></section>"}];
